{"records":[{"id":"4mb5kjywkj","title":"Western Blot raw data of the manuscript titled \"In vivo CRISPR knockout screen identifies p47 as a suppressor for HER2+ breast cancer metastasis by regulating NEMO trafficking and autophagy flux\"","description":"The raw images of WB is for the reviewers' request when reviewing the manuscript titled \"In vivo CRISPR knockout screen identifies p47 as a suppressor for HER2+ breast cancer metastasis by regulating NEMO trafficking and autophagy flux\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-12-04T00:26:32.621Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4mb5kjywkj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4mb5kjywkj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mingang","last_name":"Hao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9xkgn447vz","title":"In vivo CRISPR Screens Identify E3 Ligase Cop1 as a Modulator of Macrophage Infiltration and Cancer Immunotherapy Target","description":"Summary: Despite remarkable clinical efficacies of immune checkpoint blockade (ICB) in cancer treatment, ICB benefits in triple-negative breast cancer (TNBC) remain limited. Through pooled in vivo CRISPR knockout (KO) screens in syngeneic TNBC mouse models, we found that inhibition of the E3 ubiquitin ligase Cop1 in cancer cells decreases the secretion of macrophage-associated chemokines, reduces tumor macrophage infiltration, enhances tumor immunity and ICB response. Transcriptomics, epigenomics, and proteomics analyses revealed Cop1 functions through proteasomal degradation of the C/ebpδ protein. Cop1 substrate Trib2 functions as a scaffold linking Cop1 and C/ebpδ, which leads to polyubiquitination of C/ebpδ. Cop1 inhibition stabilizes C/ebpδ to suppress the expression of macrophage chemoattractant genes. Our integrated approach implicates Cop1 as a target for improving cancer immunotherapy efficacy by regulating chemokine secretion and macrophage infiltration in the TNBC tumor microenvironment.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-08-23T12:28:59.397Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9xkgn447vz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9xkgn447vz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Collin","last_name":"Tokheim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Binbin","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Protein Degradation","Immunotherapy","Ubiquitylation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9d5499gb8x","title":"In Vivo CRISPR Screens Identify E3 Ligase Cop1 as a Modulator of Macrophage Infiltration and Cancer Immunotherapy Target","description":"Despite remarkable clinical efficacy of immune checkpoint blockade (ICB) in cancer treatment, ICB benefits in triple-negative breast cancer (TNBC) remain limited. Through pooled in vivo CRISPR knockout (KO) screens in syngeneic TNBC mouse models, we found that deletion of the E3 ubiquitin ligase Cop1 in cancer cells decreases the secretion of macrophage-associated chemokines, reduces tumor macrophage infiltration, enhances anti-tumor immunity, and strengthens ICB response. Transcriptomics, epigenomics, and proteomics analyses revealed that Cop1 functions through proteasomal degradation of the C/ebpδ protein. Cop1 substrate Trib2 functions as a scaffold linking Cop1 and C/ebpδ, which leads to polyubiquitination of C/ebpδ. In addition, deletion of the E3 ubiquitin ligase Cop1 in cancer cells stabilizes C/ebpδ to suppress the expression of macrophage chemoattractant genes. Our integrated approach implicates Cop1 as a target for improving cancer immunotherapy efficacy in TNBC by regulating chemokine secretion and macrophage infiltration in the tumor microenvironment.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-08-24T14:43:28.246Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9d5499gb8x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9d5499gb8x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"xiaoqing","last_name":"wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vtvxykv2cr","title":"2020_Cell reports resubmission tables","description":"All tables associated with our paper titled \"High throughput CRISPR screening identifies genes involved in macrophage viability and inflammatory pathways\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-12-15T08:44:50.23Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vtvxykv2cr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vtvxykv2cr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Susan","last_name":"Carpenter","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Macrophage","Immunity","Innate Immunity","Screening","Pooled Analysis","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"sxrvzkr4hh","title":"Pooled in vitro and in vivo CRISPR-Cas9 screening identifies tumor suppressors in human colon organoids","description":"UMI validation screen, original data from in vivo tumor suppressor CRISPR/Cas9 screening experiment. Figure 6.\nExperimental setup: 281 tumor suppressor gRNAs, positive controls, neutral controls and gRNAs targeting essential genes were introduced into the CRISPR-UMI lentivirus (Michlits et al., 2017). Transduced AK organoids (APC-KO and KRASG12D) were transplanted subcutaneously in NSG mice followed by barcode sequencing of the tumor pool (from 10 mice) and 5 individual tumors. Clone and read number were determined by next generation sequencing. The conventional gRNA enrichment analysis was compared to UMI-CRISPR-screening with outlier removal.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-04-20T15:46:17.868Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/sxrvzkr4hh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/sxrvzkr4hh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Henner","last_name":"Farin","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9zn66k84n8","title":"Apatinib remodels the immunosuppressive tumor ecosystem of gastric cancer enhancing anti-PD-1 immunotherapy","description":"Apatinib has been shown to clinically enhance anti-PD-1 immunotherapy for advanced gastric cancer (GC). However, the complexity of GC immunosuppression remains a challenge for precision immunotherapy. Here, we profiled the transcriptomes of 34,182 single cells from GC patient-derived xenografts of humanized mouse models treated with vehicle, nivolumab, or nivolumab plus apatinib. Notably, excessive expression of CXCL5 in CellCycle malignant epithelium, induced by anti-PD-1 immunotherapy and blocked by combined apatinib treatment, was found to be a key driver of tumor-associated neutrophil (TAN) recruitment in tumor microenvironment through the CXCL5/CXCR2 axis. We further revealed that the protumor TAN signature was associated with anti-PD-1 immunotherapy-related progressive disease and poor cancer prognosis. Molecular and functional analyses in cell-derived xenograft models directly confirmed the positive in vivo therapeutic effect of targeting CXCL5/CXCR2 axis during anti-PD-1 immunotherapy. Altogether, our study elucidates GC immunosuppressive landscape in anti-PD-1 immunotherapy and highlights potential targets for overcoming checkpoint immunotherapy resistance.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-03-27T14:03:00.915Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9zn66k84n8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9zn66k84n8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Wangyu","last_name":"Cai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer Immunotherapy","Stomach Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"t98pjcd7t6","title":"Single-cell lineage tracing of metastatic cancer reveals selection of hybrid EMT states","description":"The underpinnings of cancer metastasis remain poorly understood, in part due to a lack of tools for probing their emergence at high resolution. Here we present macsGESTALT, an inducible CRISPR-Cas9-based lineage recorder with highly efficient single-cell capture of both transcriptional and phylogenetic information. Applying macsGESTALT to a mouse model of metastatic pancreatic cancer, we recover ~380,000 CRISPR target sites and reconstruct dissemination of ~28,000 single cells across multiple metastatic sites. We find cells occupy a continuum of epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition (EMT) states. Metastatic potential peaks in rare, late-hybrid EMT states, which are aggressively selected from a predominately epithelial ancestral pool. The gene signatures of these late-hybrid EMT states are predictive of reduced survival in both human pancreatic and lung cancer patients, highlighting their relevance to clinical disease progression. Finally, we observe evidence for in vivo propagation of S100 family gene expression across clonally distinct metastatic subpopulations.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-29T09:16:44.113Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/t98pjcd7t6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/t98pjcd7t6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kamen","last_name":"Simeonov","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Pancreatic Cancer","Metastasis","S100A Gene","Cell Lineage","Epithelial Mesenchymal Transition","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4r55hvz87r","title":"Negative DNA supercoiling induces genome-wide Cas9 off-target activity. Newton MD et al","description":"CRISPR/Cas9 is a powerful gene-editing technology, however off-target activity remains an important consideration for therapeutic applications. We have previous-ly shown that force stretching DNA induces off-target activity and hypothesised that distortions of the DNA topology in vivo, such as negative DNA supercoiling, could reduce Cas9 specificity. Using single-molecule optical-tweezers, we demonstrate that negative supercoiling λ-DNA induces sequence-specific Cas9 off-target bind-ing at multiple sites, even at low forces. Using an adapted CIRCLE-seq approach, we detect over 10,000 negative supercoiling induced Cas9 off-target double-strand breaks genome wide, caused by increased mismatch tolerance. We further demon-strate in vivo that directed local DNA distortion increases off-target activity in cells and that induced off-target events can be detected during Cas9 genome-editing. These data demonstrate that Cas9 off-target activity is regulated by DNA topology in vitro and in vivo, suggesting that cellular processes, such as transcription and replication, could induce off-target activity at previously overlooked sites. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-03-12T08:20:22.495Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4r55hvz87r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4r55hvz87r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Matt","last_name":"Newton","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Molecular Biology","Single Molecule Imaging","Next Generation Sequencing","CRISPR/Cas9","Genome Editing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ztg7pxr6ch","title":"STT3A is essential for Wnt signaling and represents a target for cancers driven by RNF43 deficiency","description":"This dataset contains the original raw and analyzed data supporting the manuscript “STT3A is essential for Wnt signaling and represents a target for cancers driven by RNF43 deficiency”. The study identifies STT3A as an essential regulator of the Wnt signaling pathway and a potential therapeutic target for RNF43-deficient cancers. Included data were generated from key experiments, including Wnt reporter assays (DDT), Western blotting (WB), and CRISPR screen, and other experiments central to the study","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-13T11:26:58.874Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ztg7pxr6ch","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ztg7pxr6ch"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhengjin","last_name":"He","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"49cw8t7km5","title":"Dataset for research article \"1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D3 synergizes with sodium selenite to inhibit ovarian cancer growth by upregulating SEPP1\"","description":"Advanced epithelial ovarian cancer lacks effective treatments.1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D3 (1,25D3) shows in vitro antitumor activity, but its clinical translation is hindered by insufficient in vivo efficacy at tolerable doses and hypercalcemia at high doses. Sodium selenite (Na2SeO3) is a safe tumor suppressor, yet its synergy with 1,25D3 in ovarian cancer remains unclear. We found Na2SeO3 synergized with 1,25D3 to inhibit ovarian cancer progression, via enhancing 1,25D3-induced VDR-SEPP1 axis activation, with SEPP1 confirmed as a direct VDR target. In vivo, this low-dose combination outperformed 1,25D3 monotherapy in suppressing tumor growth and metastasis, with no detectable toxicity. This provides a novel safe strategy for 1,25D3-based ovarian cancer therapy.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-05-13T15:31:19.394Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/49cw8t7km5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/49cw8t7km5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yanmin","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Ovarian Cancer","Selenite","Nuclear Vitamin D","Calcitriol","Selenoprotein P","Synergism"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":121238,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":137,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":121238,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":120704,"restricted":61,"embargoed":473},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2081},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":601},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":445},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":407},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":401},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":397},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":360},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":306},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":241},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":211}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":118662},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1899},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":176},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":119},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":74},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":65},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":53},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":35},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":28},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":491},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":402},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":351},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":323},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":289}]}}